let's get into audience questions  there's some really good topics this  week first one is from gila gila says  hey David how do you explain the  horribly low approval rating for  Congress and the very high reelection  rate for Congress yeah that's a good  question you would think that if  Congress if congressional approval is so  low that you would see a correspondingly  low reelection rate for members of  Congress but that's not actually the  case so I think there's two different  phenomena at play here number one is  this idea that while big picture people  say Congress is a disaster I don't  approve of the job they're doing a lot  of people simultaneously believe that  their elected officials their member of  Congress is not part of the problem or  is not that bad right so you will very  often see in other words to get an 11%  congressional approval you would imagine  that that would be sort of the average  or mean of all congressional approvals  and that thus there would be people that  are well below 11% that would get us to  that 11 percent but what it turns out is  that even though as an institution  people don't approve of Congress they  tend to approve of their own elected  officials to a much higher degree  because they apparently believe that  it's all of the other elected officials  that are the problem so that's number  one and that's sort of a basic  behavioral economics phenomena that has  been discussed a lot that's number one  number two is that the power of  incumbency allows you to raise money and  that when you have more money you're way  more likely to win so even if your  approval rating isn't that high because  you're an elected official you're going  to get contributions not only from  individuals but also from from lobbying  groups corporations etc and we know that  when you have more money you're more  likely to win so that's why and it's a  very reasonable question it's a similar  question to wait a second if our elected  officials represent us why is it that  even though most people want net  neutrality they could only get just  around half of all senators to support  it because most of their constituents  support net neutrality why wouldn't  there be more senators supporting it  it's a similar sort of thing where the  numbers don't match up with each other  and you would think if if people think  Congress is so horrible there should be  more incumbents Lewis  but it's just not the way the system is  built in fact I would argue the system  by design is one in order to consolidate  and maintain power where even with low  approval as a legislative body  individual members of Congress still  have a very good shot at being reelected  they want that in order to maintain  their power in the status quo yeah and  we also just live in a very diverse  country - I mean if you asked me if I  approve of my liberal congressman or  senator from you know Massachusetts from  Boston or Cambridge wherever I would  probably say yes okay but then if you  ask me if I approve of some senator or  congressman from Utah I would say no and  the people living there would say the  exact opposite well yeah and I think  that's part of the entire yeah I don't  agree with what agree with what the body  is doing but my specific elected  official just isn't a part of the  problem it's everybody else that's a  part of the play I like if we were in  Europe we would be consisted of maybe  like 10 countries right we're just that  diverse yes I've said this before when I  have traveled to northern Florida not  Miami Beach but northern Florida or I  was recently in rural Miami or when I've  been to California or other parts of the  country really all we have in common is  that we've decided the name of the  country is the United States of America  and ostensibly we have the same  president at all time not extensively we  have the same president although not  everybody ever recognizes that president  as their president in the same way but  really these very well could be  different countries and the cultural  differences between regions of the  United States are certainly bigger or at  least as big as the cultural differences  that exist between some of the smaller  European countries for sure but I do  think there are solutions here and you  laid out some of them and it has a lot  to do with campaign finance reform and  gerrymandering to solving that
 

